---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Video from '../components/Video.astro';

// ── PROOF ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The reel below is Vimeo 1214627867, delivered as
// "1.15.26_ChuckWhitehead_Social_Horizontal_Delivered" (47s social cut).
//
// TODO(geoff): confirm two things and I will update the caption:
//   1. Whether the speaker, Chuck Whitehead, may be named. Currently he is
//      not: the credit goes to the organiser who commissioned the work.
// Commissioned by Coldwell Banker Southern Realty (confirmed 2026-07-31).
//
// The archival footage of the talks themselves stays off this site
// permanently: that is the client's asset and the speaker's material, not a
// portfolio piece. Only the reel we produced is ours to show.
//
// mainstage-rig-ballroom.jpg (added 2026-08-20) is from a job SDub shot as a
// SUBCONTRACTOR (hired by another producer). It must stay unattributable:
// no event name, no client, no date, no duration, no attendance figures in
// the caption or alt text, ever. Capability shot only.
//
// SCOPE HONESTY: single-room coverage only. Do not claim concurrent tracks or
// breakout rooms. The offer is the main stage done properly.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Corporate Event Videography',
  description: 'Multi-camera coverage of conferences and corporate events: every session archived plus a highlight reel.',
  url: 'https://sdubmedia.com/corporate-events',
  serviceType: 'Corporate Event Videography',
  provider: { '@type': 'LocalBusiness', '@id': 'https://sdubmedia.com/#business', name: 'SDub Media' },
  areaServed: [
    { '@type': 'City', name: 'Nashville' },
    { '@type': 'City', name: 'Nolensville' },
    { '@type': 'City', name: 'Franklin' },
    { '@type': 'City', name: 'Brentwood' },
    { '@type': 'City', name: 'Murfreesboro' },
  ],
  priceRange: '$$$',
};
---
<Layout
  title="Corporate Event Video Nashville | SDub Media"
  description="Main-stage conference and corporate event video in Nashville: every talk archived with board audio and your own slides, plus a highlight reel delivered while the event is still fresh."

  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
<section>
  <div class="container-narrow text-center">
    <span class="eyebrow">For corporate events</span>
    <h1 style="text-wrap: balance;">Every talk archived. One reel you can actually use.</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; margin-top: 1.5rem;">Every main-stage session recorded end to end, plus a short highlight cut. Audio off your board. Your slides at full resolution.</p>
    <div style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Talk about your event</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="container" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">
    <img class="wedding-hero-img" fetchpriority="high" src="/images/corporate-events/camera-in-room.jpg"
         alt="A cinema camera and monitor at the back of a full conference room, speaker on stage beyond"
         width="1600" height="1200" />
  </div>
</section>

<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">What you get</span>
      <h2>Two deliverables, not one.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tiles tiles--two">
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>The Full Archive</h3>
        <p>Every session start to finish, slides cut in. Put it behind a login, send it to whoever missed it, hand it to the speaker. It is still earning next year.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>The Highlight Reel</h3>
        <p>The room filling, the speakers, the moments that landed. This is what sells next year's tickets, and it has to exist while people still remember being there.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">How we shoot it</span>
      <h2>The details that decide whether it is usable.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="tiles">
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>Audio Off the Board</h3>
        <p>A line feed from your AV desk. A camera mic in a large hall records the room, not the speaker, and no edit rescues that. It is why most conference footage goes unwatched.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>Your Slides, Not a Filmed Screen</h3>
        <p>Send the deck. We cut each slide in at full resolution, matched to the moment it appeared. Filming a projection screen is the usual approach, and it looks it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3>One Room, Done Right</h3>
        <p>We cover one room, and we would rather say so than promise four. Your main stage gets a proper multi-angle setup instead of a scramble between breakouts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* Three cameras was already claimed in passing and never explained. Saying
    "multi-camera" is what every competitor says; saying what each camera is
    FOR is not cheaply copied, and it reframes crew size from a cost the buyer
    wants to negotiate down into coverage the buyer wants more of. */}
<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Three cameras</span>
      <h2>One camera cannot be edited.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>A single locked-off camera produces a forty-five minute block that nobody watches. Not because the speaker was dull, but because with one angle there is nowhere to cut. Every trim is a visible jump. Three angles is what makes an edit possible at all.</p>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><strong>A wide that never stops rolling.</strong> Nothing is ever missing, and it is the angle everything else cuts back to</li>
      <li><strong>A tight on the speaker.</strong> This is the one you will actually use, and the one that makes a reel feel like television rather than a recording</li>
      <li><strong>One on the room.</strong> Reactions, a full house, people leaning in. It is the difference between footage of a talk and footage of an event</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Audio comes off your board on all of it, so the speaker sounds the same on every angle.</p>
    <div style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <img class="wedding-hero-img" src="/images/corporate-events/mainstage-rig-ballroom.jpg"
           alt="A camera and monitor framing a speaker on a ballroom main stage, the audience seated beyond"
           loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1200" />
      <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.75rem;">The tight angle at work on a Nashville main stage: its own monitor, audio off the board, the wide and the room camera rolling elsewhere.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">See it in action</span>
      <h2>The reel from a recent speaker event.</h2>
    </div>
    <Video id="1214627867" title="Highlight reel from a corporate speaker event" />
    <p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); margin-top: 0.75rem;">Commissioned by Coldwell Banker Southern Realty. Forty-seven seconds, cut for social. The full session was archived separately for their own library.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Stills too</span>
      <h2>Photography on the same day, by the same people.</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="text-align: center; max-width: 640px; margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;">Speakers, the room, the attendees, and the printed material nobody remembers to photograph. Organisers need these long before they need the video.</p>
    <div class="wedding-strip">
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/speaker-podium.jpg" alt="A speaker presenting from a lectern with his slide projected behind him" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1067" />
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/session-room.jpg" alt="A presenter addressing a full room during a market update session" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1067" />
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/attendees.jpg" alt="Attendees at a corporate session, laptops open and engaged" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1067" />
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/materials-detail.jpg" alt="Printed event materials held at a table" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1067" />
    </div>
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted);">Coldwell Banker Southern Realty and Coldwell Banker Global Luxury.</p>
    {/* Second strip: not every corporate event is a conference. Same
        venue/people/detail rhythm as the gala strip on /business. */}
    <div class="wedding-strip" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/outing-ballpark-wide.jpg" alt="A ballpark at golden hour seen from a private suite, a red umbrella overhead" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1066" />
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/outing-candid-laugh.jpg" alt="A guest in a Nashville jersey laughing during a company gathering at the ballpark" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1066" />
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/outing-spread-detail.jpg" alt="A guest plating hot chicken from baskets at a suite buffet" loading="lazy" width="1066" height="1600" />
    </div>
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted);">Company outings count too. A brokerage's night at the ballpark, photographed the same evening we filmed the recap. Coldwell Banker Southern Realty at First Horizon Park.</p>
    {/* Third strip: awards banquets. Same client family as the strips above
        (several of the same faces as the team portrait day on /business). */}
    <div class="wedding-strip" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/awards-flower-wall.jpg" alt="A dozen colleagues posed in front of a blue and white flower wall at an awards event" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1066" />
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/awards-handshake.jpg" alt="A congratulatory handshake across a banquet table at an awards luncheon" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1066" />
      <img src="/images/corporate-events/awards-flowers.jpg" alt="A smiling honoree holding a bouquet of white flowers at an awards luncheon" loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1066" />
    </div>
    <p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 1.5rem; color: var(--muted);">And the awards lunch where the year gets celebrated: the backdrop, the congratulations, the flowers in hand.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Why it turns around fast</span>
      <h2>The editing is the part most crews outsource.</h2>
    </div>
    <p>A reel that lands six weeks after an event has missed its job. The editing happens on our team, not in someone else's queue.</p>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li>We have run this <strong>two-output model for years</strong> on performance work: a full film for the families, a short cut for the website</li>
      <li>A conference is the <strong>same shape in a different room</strong>: one long record that must be complete, one short cut that must be good</li>
      <li>It is the same reason we can run <strong>weekly content</strong> for businesses. The edit is never the bottleneck</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="text-center" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Talk about your event</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── PRICING ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Geoff's internal model is hourly and per head: $300/hr for a lead shooter
    (photo or video), $150/hr for a second on either, $300/hr for editing.
    That model is NOT published, on purpose, and should not be. Two reasons:

      1. He asked for it to stay private (2026-07-31). Final price genuinely
         depends on crew, camera count and edit types.
      2. It argues against him. Per-head-per-hour makes three people a cost to
         negotiate down ("could two do it?"). The same money framed as three
         cameras is coverage the buyer wants more of.

    The $6,000 anchor below is derived, not invented: an 8-hour day with a
    lead plus two seconds ($4,800) and 4 hours of editing ($1,200). If the
    day runs with two LEADS (a lead videographer and a lead photographer,
    each with a second) it is $7,200, which is why the copy says "around" and
    lists what moves it rather than quoting a fixed price.

    KNOWN SOFT SPOT: 4 editing hours is optimistic for 8 hours across 3
    cameras (~24 hours of footage before a single creative decision, and the
    per-session archive cuts are on top of the reel). Flagged to Geoff
    2026-08-01. If real edit time is consistently higher, this anchor needs
    to move up, and the number here is the only place on the site to change.
    ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<section style="background: var(--soft);">
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Who shoots it</span>
      <h2>Specialists, not one person with a camera.</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem;">An event happens once. There is no second take on a keynote, which is why the people covering it do this constantly rather than occasionally.</p>
    {/* Roles only, never names or faces: the team is not willing to appear on
        the marketing site and that stays their call. These are the confirmed
        roles (see /about). Do not add one to fill a page. */}
    <ul class="facts" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
      <li>A photographer who covers <strong>live events</strong>, where you get one attempt at every moment</li>
      <li><strong>Two videographers</strong>, including the owner, so multiple angles are covered by people who know the run of show</li>
      <li><strong>Editing in house</strong>, which is the actual reason the reel comes back fast</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">More on <a href="/about">who shows up</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">What it costs</span>
      <h2>Roughly what to budget.</h2>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 1.15rem;">A full day of main-stage coverage, three cameras, audio off your board, the complete archive and one highlight reel lands <strong>around $6,000</strong> for most events.</p>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">We do not publish a rate card, because the honest answer depends on the room. A single session with a smaller crew is a much smaller number. What changes it:</p>
    <ul class="facts">
      <li><strong>How long we are rolling.</strong> One keynote is not a full day</li>
      <li><strong>How many cameras and operators.</strong> More angles means more to cut with, and more crew</li>
      <li><strong>How many edits you want back.</strong> One reel, or a cutdown for every speaker to share</li>
      <li><strong>Whether you want photography</strong> the same day, from the same team</li>
      <li><strong>How fast you need it.</strong> Turnaround depends on the event, and we will commit to a date before you book</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">Tell us the room, the run of show and what you want to do with the footage afterwards, and you will get a real number rather than a range.</p>
    <div class="text-center" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-primary">Get a quote for your event</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
</Layout>
